--- !u!114 &4598279354586381557
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 7075243568789207232}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 58cf532e110fded4caa7876c1fe3d2a1, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::Game.IDE.Presentation.DocsNodeView
  titleText: {fileID: 1567176868837139156}
  pageTitleText: {fileID: 4515360925993940223}
  descriptionText: {fileID: 6684210445670800471}
  pagesRoot: {fileID: 3453228338287174615}
  pageButtonTemplate: {fileID: 8744811284276505394}
  hideTemplate: 1
  nextButton: {fileID: 0}
  previousButton: {fileID: 0}
  selectedButtonColor: {r: 0.18, g: 0.22, b: 0.32, a: 1}
  normalButtonColor: {r: 0.06, g: 0.08, b: 0.11, a: 0.55}
  selectedTextColor: {r: 1, g: 1, b: 1, a: 1}
  normalTextColor: {r: 0.78, g: 0.82, b: 0.88, a: 1}
  title: DOCS
  pages:
  - buttonTitle: Start here
    pageTitle: Start here
    description: 'A node is not an upgrade by itself.


      A node only unlocks
      a system module.

      The module starts working only after you write code
      and press Build.


      Basic loop:

      1. Connect a node.

      2. Double
      click the node.

      3. Write module code.

      4. Press Build.

      5.
      If Build succeeds, the module becomes operational.


      If the code is missing
      or failed, the node stays installed but gives no real benefit.'
  - buttonTitle: Module
    pageTitle: 
    description: 'module declares what system part this file controls.  Example:
      module SystemCore  This means: "This code belongs to the System Core module." 
      Every working file should start with module.  Good: module Kernel module Data
      module Security  Bad: kernel code here dsadasdas system maybe  Without module,
      Build cannot understand what you are trying to create.'
  - buttonTitle: Kernel
    pageTitle: 
    description: "A required dependency means this module cannot work without another
      module.\n\nExample:\nrequire Kernel as kernel\n\nThis line tells the system:\n\n\"I
      need the Kernel module.\nIf Kernel is missing, offline, or not built successfully,\nmy
      module cannot work correctly.\"\n\nKernel is the core execution layer of the
      OS.\n\nIt controls:\n- process scheduling\n- memory usage\n- response time\n-
      crash risk\n- how many programs can run at once\n\nThe word after \"as\" is
      an alias.\n\nExample:\nrequire Kernel as kernel\n\nNow you can use Kernel data
      through:\n\nkernel.load\nkernel.scheduler\nkernel.memoryUsage\nkernel.crashRisk\n\nExample:\ntick
      every 1s {\n    systemLoad = kernel.load + data.ioLoad\n\n    if systemLoad
      > 80\n        throttle data.index by 25\n        alert \"High Load\"\n    end\n}\n\nImportant:\nrequire
      does not create the Kernel.\nIt only says that this module depends on Kernel.\n\nFor
      this dependency to work:\n1. Kernel node must exist.\n2. Kernel node must be
      connected.\n3. Kernel code must be built successfully.\n\nIf Kernel is not
      built, this module may compile,\nbut the OS will treat the dependency as unavailable.\n\nCommon
      mistake:\nrequire Kernel as kernel\n\nBut Kernel node is still Offline.\n\nThat
      means Kernel exists visually,\nbut its code is not operational yet."
  - buttonTitle: Security
    pageTitle: 
    description: "optional means this module can work without another module.\r\n\r\nExample:\r\noptional
      Security as security\r\n\r\nThis means:\r\n\"Use Security if it exists, but
      do not break if it is missing.\"\r\n\r\nExample:\r\nif security.exists\r\n   
      load security.firewall\r\nelse\r\n    warning \"No security layer\"\r\nend\r\n\r\nUse
      optional for:\r\n- Security\r\n- Control\r\n- plugins\r\n- advanced features\r\n\r\nRequired
      modules are needed.\r\nOptional modules are bonuses."
  - buttonTitle: Hardware budget
    pageTitle: Hardware budget
    description: "budget defines hardware limits for this module.\r\n\r\nExample:\r\nbudget
      {\r\n    cpu <= 72\r\n    memory <= 68\r\n    storage <= 80\r\n}\r\n\r\nThis
      tells the system:\r\n\"My module should not use more than this.\"\r\n\r\nIf
      the module exceeds budget, users may report:\r\n- lag\r\n- freezes\r\n- crashes\r\n-
      slow boot\r\n- high memory usage\r\n\r\nGood code respects hardware.\r\nBad
      code assumes infinite CPU.\r\n\r\nOld hardware can destroy even a good idea."
  - buttonTitle: Startup code
    pageTitle: Startup code
    description: "boot runs when the OS starts.\r\n\r\nExample:\r\nboot {\r\n   
      start kernel.scheduler\r\n    mount data.fileSystem\r\n}\r\n\r\nUse boot for:\r\n-
      starting services\r\n- mounting file systems\r\n- loading drivers\r\n- initializing
      modules\r\n\r\nDo not put heavy repeated work in boot.\r\n\r\nBad:\r\nboot
      {\r\n    scan allFiles\r\n    encrypt disk\r\n    rebuild index\r\n}\r\n\r\nThat
      may make startup painfully slow."
  - buttonTitle: Runtime loop
    pageTitle: Runtime loop
    description: "tick runs repeatedly while the OS is alive.\r\n\r\nExample:\r\ntick
      every 1s {\r\n    systemLoad = kernel.load + data.ioLoad\r\n\r\n    if systemLoad
      > 80\r\n        throttle data.index by 25\r\n        alert \"High Load\"\r\n   
      end\r\n}\r\n\r\nUse tick for:\r\n- checking load\r\n- reacting to problems\r\n-
      background maintenance\r\n- scheduled tasks\r\n\r\nDanger:\r\ntick every frame\r\ntick
      every 0.1s\r\n\r\nToo frequent checks can overload old hardware."
  - buttonTitle: Output values
    pageTitle: Output values
    description: "export tells the OS what values this module produces.\r\n\r\nExample:\r\nexport
      {\r\n    stability = 100 - systemLoad * 0.25\r\n    load = systemLoad\r\n}\r\n\r\nPrefer
      export over direct stat changes.\r\n\r\nBad:\r\nstability += 12\r\nnetwork
      += data.output\r\n\r\nBetter:\r\nstability = 100 - risk - load * 0.25\r\nnetwork
      = control.networkSpeed - security.firewallCost\r\n\r\nThe OS should calculate
      results from behavior, not magic numbers."
  - buttonTitle: Build result
    pageTitle: Build result
    description: 'Build checks your module code.



      If Build succeeds:

      The
      module becomes operational.



      If Build fails:

      The module stays
      installed, but it gives no real benefit.



      Warnings mean:

      The
      code works, but may create problems later.



      Errors mean:

      The
      code cannot be used.



      Common causes:

      - missing module declaration

      -
      unknown command

      - missing end

      - missing }

      - invalid structure

      -
      required module is not built'
  - buttonTitle: Why module is offline
    pageTitle: Why module is offline
    description: 'Description:

      If a module is Offline, check this:



      1.
      Is the node connected?

      2. Did you open the correct node tab?

      3.
      Does the code start with module Name?

      4. Did Build succeed?

      5.
      Does the module id match the node?

      6. Are required modules built?

      7.
      Is there an unknown statement error?



      Connected node without successful
      Build is only installed.

      It is not operational.



      Node = capability.

      Code
      = behavior.

      Build = activation.'
